---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { notes } from '@/data/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="AIM Replica | About">
  <main class="subpage">
    <section class="shell subpage__hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>What was replicated here</h1>
      <p>
        The reference site works because it behaves like a curated exhibition instead of a
        conventional portfolio: huge type, sparse navigation, precise color accents, and a smooth
        long-scroll rhythm.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="shell subpage__grid">
      <article class="subpage-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Layout</p>
        <h2>Editorial sections over card grids</h2>
        <p>
          The home page is built from a few large frames: title, manifesto, gallery access, feature
          slider, and footer. Most of the drama comes from pacing and composition, not dense content.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="subpage-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Motion</p>
        <h2>Smooth scroll with restrained reveal</h2>
        <p>
          Instead of constant micro-animation, the page favors entrance timing, modal transitions,
          image stacking, and a slightly viscous scroll feel.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="subpage-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Structure</p>
        <h2>Home, experiment, about, and overlay gallery</h2>
        <p>
          Navigation is intentionally small. The modal gallery behaves like a side route without
          forcing a full page change, preserving the exhibition flow.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="shell note-stack">
      {notes.map((note) => (
        <p>{note}</p>
      ))}
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
